<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Here again is the conjecture from the worked example, and its disproof.</p>
<p><em>Conjecture:</em> for all real numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, <m>a^3+b^3=(a+b)^3</m>.</p>
<p><em>Disproof:</em> When <m>a=4</m> and <m>b=1</m>, <m>a^3+b^3=4^3+1^3=64+1=65</m> and <m>(a+b)^3=(4+1)^3=5^3=125</m>. Since <m>65\neq 125</m>, this counterexample shows that the conjecture is false.</p>
<p>Which sentence best summarises how this solution disproves the conjecture?</p>
<div><ol marker="(1)">
<li>It substitutes <m>a=4</m> and <m>b=1</m>, and works out that <m>a^3+b^3=65</m> and <m>(a+b)^3=125</m>.</li>
<li>It shows that <m>a^3+b^3</m> and <m>(a+b)^3</m> are never equal, whatever the values of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.</li>
<li>It finds one pair of values for which the two sides differ, so the equation is not true for all <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.</li>
<li>It tries several pairs of values and finds that the two sides differ for most of them.</li>
</ol> </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>A summary of a proof names the idea that makes it work, rather than repeating the calculation. The conjecture claims that <m>a^3+b^3=(a+b)^3</m> for <em>every</em> pair of real numbers. So a single pair for which the two sides differ is enough to disprove it: that pair is a counterexample.</p>
<p>The best summary is therefore: <q>It finds one pair of values for which the two sides differ, so the equation is not true for all <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.</q></p>
<ul>
<li><p><q>It substitutes <m>a=4</m> and <m>b=1</m>, and works out that <m>a^3+b^3=65</m> and <m>(a+b)^3=125</m></q> is true, but it only describes the calculation. It does not say why the calculation disproves the conjecture.</p></li>
<li><p><q>It shows that <m>a^3+b^3</m> and <m>(a+b)^3</m> are never equal</q> is false. The solution checks one pair only, and in fact the two sides are equal when <m>a=0</m> (both are <m>b^3</m>).</p></li>
<li><p><q>It tries several pairs of values</q> misdescribes the solution, which uses one pair. One pair is all a disproof needs.</p></li>
</ul></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>(3)</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>